{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfmnTbGMuKWpb9GfmTbq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Result: Dq ≈ –430 cm⁻¹ (|Dq| ≈ 427 cm⁻¹), B ≈ 870 cm⁻¹.** This is a hand calculation, and I did not run it through a fitting routine.\n\n**Assignment.** The ground state of a d⁸ tetrahedral ion is ³T₁(F). The three spin-allowed bands are:\n- ν₁ = ³T₁(F) → ³T₂(F) = 3 600 cm⁻¹\n- ν₂ = ³T₁(F) → ³A₂(F) = 7 870 cm⁻¹\n- ν₃ = ³T₁(F) → ³T₁(P) = 16 410 cm⁻¹\n\n**Model.** Let D = |Dq| for the tetrahedral field. I took the matrix elements from memory and checked only that they reproduce the free-ion limit at Dq = 0.\n\nRelative to the ³F barycentre, the levels are:\n- ³T₂(F) lies at +2D.\n- ³A₂(F) lies at +12D.\n- The two ³T₁ levels, ³T₁(F) and ³T₁(P), mix through the matrix [[–6D, 4D], [4D, 15B]].\n\nThe lower eigenvalue of this matrix is the ground state, E_g. The upper eigenvalue is the ³T₁(P) level.\n\n**Solving.**\n1. ν₂ – ν₁ = 10D = 4 270 cm⁻¹, so D ≈ 427 cm⁻¹.\n2. ν₁ = 2D – E_g, so E_g = 854 – 3 600 = –2 746 cm⁻¹.\n3. The ³T₁ secular equation at E = E_g is (–6D – E_g)(15B – E_g) = 16D².\n4. With –6D = –2 562, this gives 184 × (15B + 2 746) ≈ 2.92×10⁶. Then 15B + 2 746 ≈ 15 850, so B ≈ 870 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Check.** These values predict ν₃ ≈ 16 040 cm⁻¹, against 16 410 cm⁻¹ observed. That is about 2% off, so the fit is reasonable.\n\nDq is negative in the tetrahedral sign convention. B ≈ 870 cm⁻¹ is about 80% of the free-ion value for Ni²⁺ (B ≈ 1 080 cm⁻¹), so the nephelauxetic reduction is plausible. I did not determine C, because three bands cannot fix it.\n\n{\"Dq\": -430, \"B\": 870, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Fit of the three tetrahedral d8 bands (3T1(F) to 3T2, 3A2 and 3T1(P)) gives |Dq| about 427 cm-1 and B about 870 cm-1, with C not determined.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":